16:14:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer is: **we already have run out**. The top-level pool of fresh, unallocated IPv4 addresses was exhausted back in 2011 at the IANA level, and the five Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) have since depleted their own free pools one by one. But the Internet didn’t break — it just forced us to stop relying on giving every device its own unique public address. Here’s what that means in practice. ### The timeline of exhaustion - **February 2011:** IANA allocated the last five /8 blocks to the RIRs, putting the global free pool at zero. - **2011–2019:** RIRs ran out in succession. APNIC (Asia-Pacific) hit exhaustion first (2011), RIPE NCC (Europe/Middle East) in 2012, LACNIC (Latin America) in 2014, ARIN (North America) in 2015, and AFRINIC (Africa) effectively ran out around 2019–2020. Today, most RIRs have no new IPv4 space to give out, only a waiting list or a market for reclaimed addresses. ### What “running out” actually looks like Nobody shut down the Internet on a specific day. Instead, it became impossible for an ISP, hosting company, or large enterprise to request a **fresh block of IPv4 addresses** from their RIR. Any new organization that needs public IPv4 space has to: - Buy addresses on the **IPv4 transfer market** (often at steep prices — a few years ago a single address could cost $25–$50). - Use **NAT (Network Address Translation)** to share a small number of public addresses among thousands of devices. - Rely on **IPv6** for native connectivity and use transition mechanisms for IPv4 reachability. ### What prevents a collapse: the technical workarounds Several long-standing technologies were already softening the blow, and they became even more critical: 1. **Private addressing + NAT** Most home/office networks use private ranges (10.x.x.x, 192.168.x.x) and a single public IP on the router. This has been standard for decades, so consumer impact was almost zero. 2. **Carrier‑Grade NAT (CGNAT)** Many ISPs now put hundreds or thousands of subscribers behind a large-scale NAT, sharing a few public addresses. This lets them add new customers without needing a new public IP for each one. The trade-off: some applications (gaming, VoIP, peer-to-peer) can break, and the “public” IP you see on a what’s‑my‑IP site might be shared with many others. 3. **The IPv4 transfer market** Companies that have surplus addresses (from legacy allocations or mergers) can sell them. Brokers facilitate transfers, and RIRs approve them. This market has kept addresses flowing to organizations that really need them, but it’s expensive and finite. 4. **IPv6 deployment** IPv6 offers an astronomically large address space. Running out of IPv4 has been the primary driver for IPv6 adoption. Modern networks are dual‑stack: they run IPv4 and IPv6 side by side. As more content and services go IPv6‑only, the pressure on IPv4 slowly decreases. ### What happens if you’re a new ISP or a cloud provider today - You cannot get a large, contiguous block of pristine IPv4 space from an RIR. - You must buy from the market (expensive, and often only small fragments). - You frequently deploy CGNAT from day one. - You heavily promote IPv6 and may even offer IPv6‑only access with translation gateways (NAT64/DNS64) so that customers can still reach legacy IPv4‑only sites. ### End‑user experience For most people, **nothing visibly changed** when the addresses ran out. Your home router still does NAT. Your phone still gets a private IP inside the carrier’s network. The only subtle signs: - Occasionally hitting rate limits or captchas because your public IP is shared with many users (CGNAT). - Some peer‑to‑peer gaming or remote access requiring extra workarounds because you’re behind multiple layers of NAT. - A slow but steady shift toward IPv6-only mobile networks in some parts of the world (like India’s Reliance Jio), where everything works transparently via NAT64. ### The bottom line Running out of IPv4 didn’t cause a crisis — it accelerated a long‑planned transition. The Internet now runs on a patchwork of address sharing, second‑hand addresses, and IPv6. The real solution to “running out” is full IPv6 adoption, but until then, NAT and markets keep the legacy IPv4 world functioning.
