16:16:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly — and that label “temporary hack” is the smoking gun. It’s the clearest admission that **nobody had a real, operational plan for what to do on the day the IPv4 address pool hit zero**. ### The “temporary” lie we all told ourselves NAT was first described in RFC 1631 (1994) with the explicit disclaimer that it was a short-term solution to buy time until IPv6 rolled out. The original authors never imagined it would become the backbone of the consumer internet. But here we are, three decades later, with NAT not only still in use but now nested inside Carrier‑Grade NAT, sometimes multiple layers deep. The “temporary hack” became so baked in that many modern protocols were redesigned to work around it, and an entire industry now depends on it. This happened because: - The engineers designing IPv6 were focused on architectural purity — end-to-end addressing, no NAT. They deliberately refused to make IPv6 a direct patch for IPv4, so it couldn’t help when the crisis hit. - Meanwhile, operators on the ground were facing immediate pressure: “We have 10,000 new customers but only 100 public IPs.” NAT solved that *now*. It didn’t matter that it was “temporary”; it worked, and IPv6 wasn’t ready (both technically and in terms of adoption). - The temporary hack **removed the urgency** to deploy IPv6. Why invest in a dual-stack rollout when you could just buy a NAT box and stretch your IPv4 addresses for another decade? The market and the engineering community effectively undercut their own plan. ### What that proves about the “plan” You’re right that calling IPv6 “the plan” is a post-hoc rationalization. A genuine plan would have included: 1. **A bridging mechanism** that allowed IPv4-only and IPv6-only devices to talk without full dual-stack (which NAT64 eventually provided, but too late and as a retrofit). 2. **Incentives or mandates** to get ISPs and content providers to deploy IPv6 *before* exhaustion, rather than waiting for the market to f